.TH MALLOC 3crunch "" "Crunch" "Crunch Library Manual"
.SH NAME
malloc \- allocate a block of memory
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <stdlib.h>
.PP
.BI "void* malloc(size_t " byte_count ");"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR malloc ()
allocates an independent block of at least
.I byte_count
bytes and returns a pointer aligned to 16 bytes.
Its contents are unspecified.
The caller must release the block with
.BR free (3crunch).
.PP
A request of zero attempts an allocation and, on success, returns a
freeable pointer. No bytes may be accessed through that pointer unless
the allocation is subsequently resized.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success, the function returns a nonnull pointer and leaves
.B errno
unchanged.
On failure, it returns null and sets
.BR errno .
.SH ERRORS
Include
.B <errno.h>
to inspect
.BR errno .
.TP
.B ENOMEM
The size is too large for the allocator, or memory could not
be obtained.
.PP
Other Linux mapping errors can also be reported.
.SH NOTES
The allocator is not thread\-safe, and Crunch
.B errno
is process\-wide.
.PP
Small blocks currently share mapped arenas; larger blocks use separate
mappings. This is an implementation detail, not permission to release
blocks with
.BR munmap
or a different allocator.
Use only
.BR free (3crunch)
or
.BR realloc (3crunch)
with pointers returned by this allocator.
Never use an interior pointer, a previously freed pointer, or a pointer
from the system C allocator.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR free (3crunch),
.BR realloc (3crunch),
.BR allocator_trim (3crunch),
.BR crunch (1)
